{"question": "A 13-month-old boy is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. Physical examination shows hepatosplenomegaly. A venous blood sample obtained for routine screening tests is milky. After refrigeration, a creamy supernatant layer appears on top of the sample. Genetic analysis shows a mutation in the apolipoprotein C-II gene (APOC2) on chromosome 19. This patient is at greatest risk for developing which of the following complications?", "options": {"A": "Acute pancreatitis", "B": "Myocardial infarction", "C": "Corneal arci", "D": "Cholesterol embolization syndrome", "E": "Cerebrovascular accident"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Hepatology", "Genetics", "Lipid Metabolism", "Inborn Errors of Metabolism"], "option_domains": ["Hematologic Disorders", "Lipid Metabolism"], "question_analyses": {"Pediatrics": "The scenario described involves a 13-month-old boy with hepatosplenomegaly and milky blood samples, which upon refrigeration show a creamy supernatant layer. The genetic analysis reveals a mutation in the apolipoprotein C-II gene (APOC2). This mutation is associated with a condition known as chylomicron retention disease (CRD), also referred to as chylomicronemia syndrome. ### Diagnosis and Explanation:\nChylomicron retention disease is a rare genetic disorder characterized by the accumulation of chylomicrons (lipoproteins that transport dietary fats) in the blood. The milky appearance of the blood sample is due to the presence of chylomicrons, which are not properly cleared from the blood. The genetic mutation in APOC2 affects the function of apolipoprotein C-II, a key enzyme that regulates the breakdown of chylomicrons. This leads to the accumulation of chylomicrons in the blood, causing hepatosplenomegaly (enlargement of the liver and spleen) and other complications. ### Complications:\nThe primary complications associated with chylomicron retention disease include:\n1. **Hepatomegaly and Splen